---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="How Verdict decides"
  description="What Verdict measures, how the signals combine, and where it fails."
>
  <h1>How Verdict decides</h1>
  <p>
    Verdict estimates. It does not know. A high score means the pattern of reviews on this
    listing resembles patterns we have seen on listings that were openly solicited for paid
    reviews. It is evidence, not a report, and it is wrong sometimes. The numbers below tell
    you how often, once they exist. Where they do not exist yet, this page says so rather than
    guessing.
  </p>

  <h2>What we measure</h2>
  <p>Six signals. Every one is deterministic: no language model runs at analysis time.</p>

  <h3>1. Rating distribution deconvolution</h3>
  <p>
    Genuine ratings for a product category settle into a shape. Manipulated ratings pile up at
    four and five stars on top of that shape. This signal fits how much of the observed
    histogram looks like an injection on top of the organic shape, using constrained least
    squares.
  </p>
  <p class="worked-example">
    Worked example: an organic shape of <span class="data">10% / 10% / 20% / 30% / 30%</span>
    across one to five stars, with an observed histogram of
    <span class="data">5% / 8% / 12% / 35% / 40%</span>, fits an injected share of
    <span class="data">28.6%</span> with a residual error of <span class="data">0.027</span>.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Temporal burst detection</h3>
  <p>
    Review arrivals are treated as a point process. A rolling 28 day median sets the baseline
    rate, and any day exceeding the 99th percentile of a Poisson distribution at that rate is
    flagged. Adjacent flagged days merge into one burst.
  </p>
  <p class="worked-example">
    Worked example: 30 days steady at 2 reviews a day, then a 3 day spike to 50 reviews a day,
    then back to 2. Verdict flags one burst spanning those 3 days, holding
    <span class="data">150</span> reviews, about <span class="data">67%</span> of the whole
    series.
  </p>
  <p>
    Weak alone. Launches, discounts, and press coverage all produce legitimate bursts. It only
    carries weight alongside the next signal.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Conditional verification concentration</h3>
  <p>
    Not the raw unverified share, which is noisy and varies by category. Instead, whether
    unverified reviews concentrate exactly where manipulation would put them: five star reviews
    arriving inside a burst.
  </p>
  <p class="worked-example">
    Worked example: if reviews inside a five star burst are unverified twice as often as the
    listing's reviews overall, the lift is <span class="data">2.0</span>. A lift near
    <span class="data">1.0</span> is unremarkable.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Listing identity drift</h3>
  <p>
    Sellers sometimes reuse an existing listing for a different product, so its reviews
    describe something else entirely. This signal compares review text against the current
    product title and category using a small sentence embedding model, and looks for a change
    point in that comparison over time.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is the most persuasive line Verdict can produce, and also the one with the biggest
    open cost: a bundled embedding model is 20 to 25 MB, against an 8 MB budget for the whole
    extension. Whether version 0.1 ships it or defers it to 0.2 has not been decided yet, so it
    does not run today.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Text near duplication</h3>
  <p>
    Reviews that repeat each other almost word for word are grouped using MinHash over
    character five grams and locality sensitive hashing, then clustered above 0.7 Jaccard
    similarity.
  </p>
  <p class="worked-example">
    Worked example: five reviews, three of them byte identical and two genuinely different,
    cluster into one group holding <span class="data">60%</span> of the reviews.
  </p>
  <p>
    Review farms increasingly generate text with language models, so near duplication is a
    decaying signal. It stays in the model with low weight because it still catches the cheap
    end of the market. It is not the foundation.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Reviewer graph</h3>
  <p>
    A farm serves many sellers, so its accounts co-review unrelated products. This signal
    projects a bipartite reviewer-product graph onto a reviewer-reviewer graph, extracts the
    statistically significant edges, and scores the resulting communities. It needs
    cross-product visibility, so it is opt in and ships in version 0.2, not 0.1.
  </p>

  <h2>How the signals combine</h2>
  <p>
    The plan is logistic regression on the six feature values, moving to gradient boosting only
    if it improves held out performance and the model stays calibrated. Every logistic
    regression coefficient is explainable and exports to a few kilobytes of JSON. Isotonic
    regression calibrates the raw output into a probability on a held out slice.
  </p>
  <p>
    Nothing is trained yet. This section will publish the actual coefficients once a labelled
    corpus exists to train and calibrate against.
  </p>

  <h2>How well it works</h2>
  <p>
    Not published yet. This section will carry precision, recall, and a calibration curve
    against a held out set that is never touched during development, as absolute numbers, not
    adjectives, once that evaluation has been run.
  </p>

  <h2>Where it fails</h2>
  <p>Written before anyone asks, because these are the honest edges of what this method can do.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>New listings with too few reviews to say anything. Verdict shows "not enough data" rather than a number below its minimum thresholds.</li>
    <li>Categories with genuinely unusual rating shapes, like replacement parts, where the organic prior itself is hard to pin down.</li>
    <li>Products that went viral and look bursty for entirely honest reasons.</li>
    <li>Manipulation sophisticated enough to be indistinguishable from organic behaviour at the sample size a single listing provides.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How the labels were built</h2>
  <p>
    Not published yet. The corpus methodology, including how review solicitation groups were
    read to find genuine positive labels, will be published here in full, because a claim about
    accuracy is worth nothing without it.
  </p>
</Layout>

